  "u2-ns1": passage("u2-ns1", "Religion and learning under the Mongols",
    "Mongol rulers treated religion as a matter of state. Chinggis Khan freed the clergy of every faith from taxes and labor service in return for their prayers for the khan, and his successors kept the policy. At Mongke Khan's court in Karakorum in 1254, Buddhist, Muslim, and Christian teachers debated before the khan's officials. The khans themselves came to follow different faiths: Khubilai favored Tibetan Buddhism, while the rulers of the Ilkhanate and the Golden Horde converted to Islam, in 1295 and in the early 1300s. Mongol rule also mixed artistic and scientific traditions. In Ilkhanid Persia, the minister Rashid al-Din wrote a history of the world with help from Chinese, Indian, and European informants, and Persian painters adopted Chinese ways of drawing clouds, mountains, and dragons. Muslim astronomers worked at the Yuan court in China, and Chinese physicians served in Persia."),
