  "u2-ns10": passage("u2-ns10", "From the Indian Ocean to Venice",
    "The spice trade linked distant regions in a long chain. Pepper grown in southern India was sold in Calicut to Arab and Gujarati merchants, who shipped it to Aden, at the mouth of the Red Sea. From there, Egyptian merchants took it by ship and caravan to Cairo and Alexandria, where the Mamluk sultans taxed it heavily. Venetian merchants bought it in Alexandria and sold it across Europe at many times the price paid in India. Each link in the chain added profit and taxes, and no single merchant traveled the whole route. Europeans' wish to reach the spice lands without paying the Mamluks and the Venetians helped drive Portuguese voyages around Africa in the late 1400s."),
  "u2-ns11": passage("u2-ns11", "Songhai and the Saharan trade",
    "As Mali weakened in the 1400s, the city of Gao on the Niger River became the center of a new state, Songhai. Sunni Ali (ruled about 1464 to 1492) built a fleet of river boats and a cavalry army, took Timbuktu in 1468 and Djenné a few years later, and made Songhai the largest state in West Africa. His successor Askia Muhammad, who made the pilgrimage to Mecca in 1496 and 1497, governed through appointed officials, supported Muslim scholars in Timbuktu, and kept control of the trade routes north across the Sahara. Like Ghana and Mali before it, Songhai drew much of its wealth from taxing the gold and salt that passed through its markets."),
  "u2-ns12": passage("u2-ns12", "How religions traveled",
    "Religions spread along the same routes as goods. Buddhism had reached China and Southeast Asia centuries earlier through merchants and monks, and by 1200 it had largely died out in India, its land of origin, while it flourished in East and Southeast Asia. Islam spread in the period c. 1200 to c. 1450 in a similar way. Muslim merchants settled in port cities from East Africa to Sumatra and Java, married local women, and built mosques; local rulers who converted gained trading partners and the prestige of a world faith. In West Africa, kings and merchants in trading cities became Muslim long before most villagers did. In both cases, the new faith often blended with older beliefs, and it took root first in cities and courts and only later in the countryside."),
